WEBVTT

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:03.752
{happy}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:05.793
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:05.793 --> 00:00:09.996
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:16.899
A crypto exchange with hundreds of millions of registered users has just lost six

00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:17.808
point three billion dollars.

00:00:17.808 --> 00:00:19.628
Most of it left in about twenty minutes.

00:00:19.628 --> 00:00:21.185
How does that happen?

00:00:21.185 --> 00:00:23.575
And who can get any of it back?

00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:28.680
I'm Mittal Shah, and today I'll take you through the biggest crypto theft ever

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.340
recorded.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.981
{fluent}The exchange is Binance, the largest in the world.

00:00:32.981 --> 00:00:38.453
Late on Thursday, the twenty-ninth of October, money began leaving its wallets on

00:00:38.453 --> 00:00:40.540
ten different blockchains at once.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.215
Blockchain analysis firms say most of it moved in about twenty minutes.

00:00:45.215 --> 00:00:49.205
Binance says about three billion dollars has since been frozen.

00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:50.592
It has not said what happened to the rest.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:56.426
The earlier record was a theft of one and a half billion dollars from another

00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:59.502
exchange, Bybit, in February last year.

00:00:59.502 --> 00:01:01.553
This is about four times bigger.

00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:07.128
Binance says customer money is safe and that it will cover the losses itself.

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:10.715
It paused withdrawals, and said it planned to restart them within about two days.

00:01:11.309 --> 00:01:13.895
Now, how does a theft like this work?

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:15.695
Start with the wallets.

00:01:15.695 --> 00:01:20.251
An exchange keeps most of its coins offline, in what is called cold storage.

00:01:20.251 --> 00:01:25.083
But it keeps some connected to the internet, so customers can withdraw money any

00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:25.772
time.

00:01:25.772 --> 00:01:27.839
That is a hot wallet.

00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:31.462
Think of the cash drawer at a shop, as opposed to the safe in the back.

00:01:32.005 --> 00:01:38.051
Every move out of a wallet needs a digital signature, made with a secret key.

00:01:38.051 --> 00:01:42.071
It works like a signature on a cheque.

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:43.620
Here is the key point:

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.132
the analysts who studied the thefts say that the attacker did not break into one

00:01:47.132 --> 00:01:50.094
wallet at a time.

00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:54.802
They think the attacker got control of the system that signs the transactions, so it

00:01:54.802 --> 00:01:59.285
would sign whatever the attacker asked, one blockchain after another.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:02.157
That is not the same as robbing one drawer.

00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:06.574
It is closer to getting hold of the machine that signs every cheque.

00:02:06.574 --> 00:02:08.468
This is the analysts' reading.

00:02:08.468 --> 00:02:11.632
Binance has not published its own explanation yet.

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:16.785
It first said an engineer's laptop may have been hacked, and later said it had found

00:02:16.785 --> 00:02:17.940
more points of breach.

00:02:18.511 --> 00:02:23.131
Analysts also say the preparation began at least a day and a half earlier, with

00:02:23.131 --> 00:02:24.896
small test transfers.

00:02:24.896 --> 00:02:26.178
So this was planned, not a lucky break.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:27.930
Who did it?

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:33.165
Two blockchain analysis firms say, with high confidence, that it was TraderTraitor,

00:02:33.165 --> 00:02:34.960
a hacking unit linked to North Korea.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.402
The same group was blamed by the United States FBI for the Bybit theft.

00:02:40.402 --> 00:02:42.307
But this is their assessment.

00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:46.103
Binance has not named anyone, and North Korea routinely denies such attacks.

00:02:46.728 --> 00:02:48.049
Now, the freezing.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:51.122
This is the part that surprises people.

00:02:51.122 --> 00:02:53.484
Some of the stolen money was in stablecoins.

00:02:53.484 --> 00:03:00.129
A stablecoin is a digital coin designed to hold a steady value, because it is pegged

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:01.262
to a currency.

00:03:01.262 --> 00:03:06.992
Most of the big ones are pegged to the dollar, but a stablecoin can be pegged to the

00:03:06.992 --> 00:03:09.483
euro or any other currency too.

00:03:09.483 --> 00:03:13.221
The companies that issue them can block particular wallets.

00:03:13.221 --> 00:03:18.341
Within an hour, Tether and Circle did exactly that, and analysts say the issuers

00:03:18.341 --> 00:03:21.301
froze around two point seven billion dollars in total.

00:03:21.825 --> 00:03:23.996
But bitcoin and ether work differently.

00:03:23.996 --> 00:03:25.180
No company runs them.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.703
There is no one to call.

00:03:26.703 --> 00:03:32.245
Analysts say billions of dollars in those coins are beyond any freeze.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.258
And the thief did not sit still:

00:03:34.258 --> 00:03:38.574
reports say some stolen coins were quickly sold at a discount, to turn them into

00:03:38.574 --> 00:03:41.019
other assets before they could be frozen.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:42.579
So what comes next?

00:03:42.579 --> 00:03:48.073
Analysts expect the money to be passed through cross-chain bridges, swap services,

00:03:48.073 --> 00:03:49.299
and cash-out brokers.

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:53.468
Investigators will try to follow it, and more freezes are possible.

00:03:53.468 --> 00:04:00.173
Binance's own emergency fund is more than six times smaller than the loss, so how it

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.317
covers the cost is something to watch.

00:04:02.317 --> 00:04:05.533
So is the explanation of how the attacker got in.

00:04:06.102 --> 00:04:10.455
We do not know yet how the signing system was compromised, how much will be

00:04:10.455 --> 00:04:15.022
recovered, or whether the attribution will be confirmed by officials.

00:04:15.022 --> 00:04:16.158
{happy}To sum up:

00:04:16.158 --> 00:04:21.758
an exchange can hold the keys to thousands of customers' coins in one place, and

00:04:21.758 --> 00:04:25.144
when that single point of control is compromised, the loss can be enormous.

00:04:25.144 --> 00:04:31.559
Some of it can be frozen, but only where a company is in charge of the coin.

00:04:31.559 --> 00:04:34.522
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

